<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/developer/msbuild/2003">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <MSBuildAllProjects>$(MSBuildAllProjects);$(MSBuildThisFileFullPath)</MSBuildAllProjects>
    <HasSharedItems>true</HasSharedItems>
    <SharedGUID>a1b2c3d4-e5f6-4a7b-8c9d-bed2c0000001</SharedGUID>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <PropertyGroup Label="Configuration">
    <Import_RootNamespace>Birko.Redis</Import_RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Two forms, one dependency. Under Central Package Management a PackageReference must NOT
         carry a Version (NU1008) — the consumer's Directory.Packages.props owns it. Three Birko
         consumers use CPM, so a version-carrying declaration made their solutions unrestorable.
         Conditioning on the well-known $(ManagePackageVersionsCentrally) serves both.

         This project wraps StackExchange.Redis, so it owns the dependency: a consumer that imports
         this projitems gets the driver without ever learning its package name. That is the point of
         the framework, and it costs a consumer importing only a subset nothing — measured (TASK-234):
         a PackageReference inside a .projitems materialises ONLY in a project that imports that
         .projitems, so an ElasticSearch consumer resolves NEST and no Redis, and this one resolves
         StackExchange.Redis and no NEST.

         Three siblings — BackgroundJobs.Redis, Caching.Redis, MessageQueue.Redis — build on this
         project and therefore do NOT declare the driver: a consumer imports them alongside this one,
         and two PackageReference items in one project file is NU1504, an error under the
         -warnaserror the build sweep runs.

         Birko.Health.Redis is the deliberate exception and is documented in its own projitems. It
         uses the driver directly and needs nothing from here, so it neither declares (it would
         collide wherever both are imported — Birko.Sandbox imports both) nor takes a dependency on
         this project (that would drag Configuration, Data.Core, Data.Stores, Contracts and Time into
         a consumer that wanted one health check). It documents the package instead.

         Floats within the major rather than pinning, and TASK-234 measured why: the float moved this
         family 2.8 -> 2.13 and immediately surfaced two latent breaks a pin had been hiding — an
         overload of StringSetAsync that no longer bound, and seven tests whose Moq setups named
         superseded StreamAdd / StreamReadGroup signatures. -->
    <PackageReference Include="StackExchange.Redis" Version="2.*" Condition="'$(ManagePackageVersionsCentrally)' != 'true'" />
    <PackageReference Include="StackExchange.Redis" Condition="'$(ManagePackageVersionsCentrally)' == 'true'" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)RedisSettings.cs" />
    <Compile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)RedisConnectionManager.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
